DATABASE_MIGRATIONS.sql
add_stripe_price_id.sql
*.sql
/extra
/src/scripts/*
# …but the OPERATIONAL scripts must ship: the backfills are deliverables the
# billing tickets require, and the CLI entry points are documented in
# docs/BILLING_RUNBOOK.md as the way to run and pause these jobs. An on-call
# runbook that references a script nobody else has is not a runbook.
#
# (The scripts already tracked stayed tracked because git ignores .gitignore for
# files already in the index — which is why this was not noticed sooner.)
#
# The rule above is `/src/scripts/*`, NOT `/src/scripts`. That distinction is
# load-bearing: git does not descend into an excluded DIRECTORY, so every
# negation below was inert while the rule named the directory itself. Excluding
# the contents leaves the directory readable, and the negations take effect.
# A new operational script needs a line here AND will otherwise be silently
# dropped from the deploy while its npm entry point remains in package.json.)
!/src/scripts/print-migrations.ts
!/src/scripts/backfill-default-payment-methods.ts
!/src/scripts/backfill-billing-transactions.ts
!/src/scripts/backfill-stale-cancellations.ts
!/src/scripts/check-ledger-gaps.ts
!/src/scripts/run-notification-jobs.ts
!/src/scripts/drain-email-outbox.ts
!/src/scripts/backfill-credit-purchase-receipts.ts
!/src/scripts/backfill-credit-expiry.ts
!/src/scripts/backfill-next-billing-date.ts
# Ships despite being a development aid: package.json registers `seed:expiring`,
# and an npm entry point pointing at a file that was silently dropped from the
# deploy is the exact failure this whole block exists to prevent. It grants
# credits nobody paid for, so it demands an explicit --user, tags every lot it
# creates, and --remove deletes exactly those.
!/src/scripts/seed-expiring-credits.ts
/claude reference
